uest rather than offering generic caution.\n- Every sentence must be one line, at most 55 words, and end with at least one\n  allowed citation. Use each finding at most once and return at most four\n  patches.\n- Do not add headings, lists, sections, commentary, or an entire worked\n  example. Do not rewrite, delete, merge, split, or reorder any other line.\n- Omit a finding when its evidence does not directly support a compact patch.\n  An empty patches array is successful.\n", "packet": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\nI'm currently working on a project. The professor wasn't clear, she just said that I will use NLP techniques on some data, de-identified Electronic Health Records, to extract Asian subgroups, and try to abstract the same subgroups as are in CDC WONDER data. Then, I'll apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases. I have no clue what she means. Can you first help explain what she might mean? I don't know examples of what Electronic Health Records look like. What NLP techniques might I be using on data like this? What subgroups in CDC WONDER data would I be abstracting? How could I apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases? What skills should I brush up on? Please help me create a plan for my research going forward, according to what the professor said.\n\nNUMBERED ACCEPTED DRAFT — PRESERVE UNPATCHED LINES\nDRAFT WORDS: 1026; MAXIMUM NET PATCH GROWTH: 0 WORDS; FINAL CAP: 1024 WORDS\n1: Your professor probably means: turn free-text clues in de-identified Electronic Health Records, or EHRs, into standardized Asian-subgroup variables matching a specified CDC WONDER classification, then compare the proportion of eligible patients meeting validated autoimmune-disease definitions across those groups. \n2: An EHR is a computerized longitudinal record containing structured fields such as demographics, diagnosis codes, medications and laboratory results, plus unstructured narrative notes; NLP converts relevant narrative into analyzable fields. [shard_03549_2575] [shard_03193_44799]\n3: Here “abstract” means extract and normalize varied expressions to controlled labels, while autoimmune diseases are conditions in which immunity attacks healthy cells, including rheumatoid arthritis and systemic lupus erythematosus. [shard_01866_16930]\n4: Treat race, ethnicity, ancestry and national origin as related but noninterchangeable social variables, retain each label’s wording and source, and never interpret an administrative category as biological ancestry. [shard_03064_71833]\n5: A synthetic structured record might read “patient_id=P1042; race=Asian; ethnicity=Not Hispanic; detailed_race=Filipino; language=Tagalog,” where “Filipino” is direct subgroup evidence but “Tagalog” alone is not. \n6: A synthetic note might say, “Patient self-identifies as Filipino American; rheumatology follows her for systemic lupus,” making “Filipino American” and “systemic lupus” the exact spans to extract. \n7: For P1042, the pipeline would normalize “Filipino American” to Filipino, combine lupus text with structured diagnostic and clinical evidence, merge duplicate encounters to one patient, and count that patient once in the Filipino lupus numerator. \n8: Before NLP, audit whether de-identification preserved detailed demographics, original note types, usable dates, ICD codes, medications, laboratories, patient and encounter identifiers, and variables defining active care. \n9: CDC WONDER is a collection of separately documented query databases rather than one dataset, so the professor must identify the intended database, release and codebook. [shard_05750_57588]\n10: If she means vital-statistics mortality data, the historically prominent death-certificate subgroups are Asian Indian, Chinese, Filipino, Japanese, Korean and Vietnamese, while newer single-race tabulations can include Other Asian and expose as many as 31 race categories. [shard_00350_78120] [shard_03064_71833] [shard_04312_21837]\n11: The Multiple Cause of Death database uses ICD-10-coded underlying and contributing causes, covers national through county residence, and supplies denominators for crude and age-adjusted mortality rates, not autoimmune prevalence. [shard_04026_74031] [shard_01519_72088]\n12: Single-race results begin in 2018, bridged-race results end in 2020, and those series should not be joined as though their classifications were unchanged. [shard_04312_21837]\n13: Counts below ten are generally suppressed, detailed county denominators can prevent age-adjusted estimates, and exports should retain query notes because automated access and reproducibility are constrained. [shard_03758_50956] [shard_03567_55288] [shard_00293_53563]\n14: Begin NLP with an interpretable dictionary of approved labels, spelling variants and abbreviations, plus regular expressions for self-identification, negation, family references and historical mentions. \n15: Clinical named-entity recognition, or NER, marks subgroup mentions and contextual attributes, while normalization maps expressions such as “Filipino-American” to the agreed CDC category. [shard_02454_21127]\n16: A supervised classifier can exploit broader context but requires manually labeled examples, whereas an LLM may reduce initial engineering yet introduces privacy, hallucination and reproducibility risks and must remain in an approved secure environment. [shard_03468_57413] [shard_02484_70922] [shard_04699_27397]\n17: Preserve multiracial identities as multiple labels or a prespecified category, distinguish explicit self-report from staff-entered or inferred evidence, and assign unknown when evidence is absent. \n18: Do not classify from names, language or geography without explicit approval and validation because these proxies overlap and can misclassify people. [shard_03655_15858] [shard_05619_19173]\n19: Prevalence is the proportion already affected: point prevalence uses cases present on one index date over everyone eligible then, period prevalence uses anyone affected during an interval over that interval’s average eligible population, and incidence instead counts new onsets among people initially disease-free. [shard_04604_9387] [shard_04300_84874]\n20: A defensible one-year denominator might be unique adults with at least one encounter during 2025 and sufficient prior observation, stratified by extracted subgroup, rather than every historical chart. \n21: For lupus, pilot repeated M32 diagnoses or rheumatologist documentation plus supporting antimalarial, ANA, dsDNA or complement evidence; for rheumatoid arthritis, pilot repeated M05/M06 diagnoses plus rheumatology text, disease-modifying medication or rheumatoid-factor evidence. [shard_06487_58913] [shard_06123_32469] [shard_02084_76011]\n22: If 12 distinct Filipino patients meet the lupus phenotype among 600 eligible Filipino patients, period prevalence is 12/600=0.020, or 2.0%, reported with a binomial 95% confidence interval and suppressed under the approved small-cell rule when necessary. \n23: Estimate crude and age/sex-standardized prevalence, never substitute WONDER’s national mortality denominator for the health system’s EHR denominator, and state that results describe the care population because EHR inclusion depends on health-care use. [shard_01425_9658]\n24: A large English EHR study measured rheumatoid-arthritis incidence and point prevalence, but I could not establish a published autoimmune-prevalence study jointly covering the detailed U.S. Asian categories above. [shard_02084_76011]\n25: Write an annotation manual covering allowable evidence, normalization, multiracial cases, uncertainty, negation, relatives and missingness, then have two reviewers independently annotate a stratified sample, calculate agreement, adjudicate disagreements and freeze a gold standard. [shard_06007_6716] [shard_02132_74384]\n26: Split by patient, not note, into training, validation and untouched test sets, and report precision, recall and F1 overall and within every subgroup because accuracy can conceal minority-class failures. [shard_02858_24324]\n27: Validate three separate links: subgroup extraction against human labels, autoimmune phenotypes against specialist chart review, and prevalence against denominator eligibility and deduplication. \n28: Run sensitivity analyses for missing subgroup data, uncertain mappings, phenotype strictness and plausible misclassification. \n29: Obtain the institution’s IRB or human-subjects determination and privacy approval even for de-identified records, documenting whether HIPAA Safe Harbor or Expert Determination applies, maintaining least-privilege access, suppressing small cells and prohibiting re-identification. [shard_02489_8521] [shard_03621_76660]\n30: Interpret differences as potentially reflecting documentation, access, selection, coding and institutional geography—not biological causation—and avoid treating Asian populations as homogeneous. [shard_06100_7595]\n31: Use weeks 1–2 for governance, the data dictionary and a professor-approved WONDER crosswalk; weeks 3–4 for SQL extraction and an annotation pilot; weeks 5–7 for rules or NER development; weeks 8–9 for test-set and chart-review validation; and weeks 10–12 for prevalence estimates, sensitivity analyses and reporting. \n32: Brush up on SQL, Python, pandas, regular expressions, ICD-10-CM, clinical terminology, NER and classification, confusion-matrix metrics, prevalence denominators, confidence intervals and age standardization. \n33: A practical tool stack is PostgreSQL for querying, Label Studio for annotation, cTAKES or CLAMP for clinical NLP, and R for prevalence analysis. [shard_01697_66283] [shard_02454_21127]\n34: Ask the professor which WONDER database, years and subgroup labels she means; whether only explicit self-identification is allowed; which notes and structured fields exist; which diseases, phenotype definitions and observation window she wants; how active patients are defined; and what validation sample, suppre